  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Slightly cooler and drier conditions are \nforecast for today, before showers and thunderstorms return \nSunday.\n\nHigh pressure currently centered across southern Quebec will\ncontinue to build southeastward over the course of the day\ntoday, eventually reaching the Gulf of Maine by this evening. \nAs the high shifts further east, winds will gradually shift from\nout of the north/northeast this morning to out of the east and \nthen southeast this afternoon. Persistent cool/dry advection has\nenabled dewpoints to drop into the upper 50s to mid 60s as of \nearly this morning. With this somewhat drier airmass in place, \nzonal flow aloft, and the influence of the high building to our\nnorth, conditions are expected to remain dry through the day \ntoday. Temperatures are forecast to climb into the 70s to near \n80 beneath mostly cloudy skies.\n\nHigh pressure will shift further offshore tomorrow while a\nshortwave disturbance tracks eastward across southern Ontario\nand Quebec. Winds at the surface will turn southerly in response\nto the high moving further offshore, while winds aloft turn\nwesterly in response to the shortwave passing to our north. The\nnet effect of these wind shifts will be to draw a warmer, more\nhumid airmass back into the area from the south and west.\nTemperatures are forecast to climb well into the 80s with\ndewpoints jumping back into the lower 70s. Model guidance \nsuggests that chances for showers and potentially a few \nthunderstorms will increase tomorrow afternoon into tomorrow \nnight as a cold front approaches from the north, moving into the\nwarm and humid airmass. This cold front will eventually move \nthrough the area Sunday night, causing winds to shift to out of \nthe north to northeast.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily chances for showers persist into next \nweek along with cooler temperatures. \n\nUpper-level ridging breaks down early next week, with multiple \nshortwave disturbances moving across the Mid-Atlantic in the days \nfollowing. Mid-level moisture brought in from the south will \noverrun moist northeasterly flow off the Atlantic, leading to\ndaily chances for showers. Temperatures remain somewhat uncertain\ndue to model discrepancy in cloud cover, but they continue to \nfavor the cooler side of guidance given a possible cold air \ndamming surface pattern. \n\nWell below average temperatures could last through midweek as nearby \ndisturbances continue to bring rain and showers to the area. Towards \nthe bay and coastlines, wind gusts could remain elevated through \nlate next week as these disturbances shift offshore. Overall, \ncoastal flooding could become something to watch out for next\nweek. See the coastal flooding section below for more details.",
